WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.784
<v Policy Analyst>Can I ask you something that might sound odd? How

00:00:02.784 --> 00:00:05.568
<v Policy Analyst>do people here find out about project leads? I keep

00:00:05.568 --> 00:00:08.352
<v Policy Analyst>hearing someone got one, but I never see them posted.

00:00:08.702 --> 00:00:12.528
<v Senior Policy Specialist>Oh, that's easy. You just mention it to the director

00:00:12.528 --> 00:00:15.206
<v Senior Policy Specialist>after the monthly meeting. Everyone knows that.

00:00:15.556 --> 00:00:18.773
<v Policy Analyst>I didn't know that. And I'm usually remote on meeting

00:00:18.773 --> 00:00:21.991
<v Policy Analyst>days, so I join by video and leave when it

00:00:21.991 --> 00:00:25.208
<v Policy Analyst>ends. Where I worked before, walking up to a director

00:00:25.208 --> 00:00:27.460
<v Policy Analyst>uninvited would have been seen as overstepping.

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:31.033
<v Senior Policy Specialist>Huh. I never thought of it as a rule. It

00:00:31.033 --> 00:00:34.256
<v Senior Policy Specialist>just seemed like how things work. I learned it my

00:00:34.256 --> 00:00:36.834
<v Senior Policy Specialist>first year from a supervisor who's long retired.

00:00:37.184 --> 00:00:40.110
<v Policy Analyst>That's what makes it hard. To you it's common sense.

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:43.035
<v Policy Analyst>To me it was invisible. Is there anything else like

00:00:43.035 --> 00:00:43.328
<v Policy Analyst>that?

00:00:43.678 --> 00:00:47.297
<v Senior Policy Specialist>Let me think. When the director says, "That should work,"

00:00:47.297 --> 00:00:50.916
<v Senior Policy Specialist>it often means she has doubts. Most people here wait

00:00:50.916 --> 00:00:52.726
<v Senior Policy Specialist>for a follow-up before acting.

00:00:53.076 --> 00:00:56.436
<v Policy Analyst>I took that as a yes last month and started

00:00:56.436 --> 00:00:58.788
<v Policy Analyst>drafting. That explains the awkward meeting afterward.

00:00:59.138 --> 00:01:02.622
<v Senior Policy Specialist>I'm sorry. Nobody meant to leave you out. Those habits

00:01:02.622 --> 00:01:06.107
<v Senior Policy Specialist>started for good reasons. People wanted to be polite and

00:01:06.107 --> 00:01:08.546
<v Senior Policy Specialist>avoid putting the director on the spot.

00:01:08.896 --> 00:01:11.799
<v Policy Analyst>I believe that. The politeness is one of the things

00:01:11.799 --> 00:01:14.702
<v Policy Analyst>I like here. I just need to know what the

00:01:14.702 --> 00:01:17.606
<v Policy Analyst>signals mean, and I need to know when an opportunity

00:01:17.606 --> 00:01:17.896
<v Policy Analyst>exists.

00:01:18.246 --> 00:01:22.068
<v Senior Policy Specialist>That's fair. Researchers have found that when criteria aren't written

00:01:22.068 --> 00:01:25.890
<v Senior Policy Specialist>down, people tend to pick whoever they already have in

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:28.566
<v Senior Policy Specialist>mind. Writing them first takes that away.

00:01:28.916 --> 00:01:32.025
<v Policy Analyst>Would it help if openings were posted with what counts

00:01:32.025 --> 00:01:35.133
<v Policy Analyst>and when decisions are made? Evening staff probably miss them

00:01:35.133 --> 00:01:35.444
<v Policy Analyst>too.

00:01:35.794 --> 00:01:39.657
<v Senior Policy Specialist>I think so. I'll suggest it to the supervisor. And

00:01:39.657 --> 00:01:43.520
<v Senior Policy Specialist>I'll be clearer myself. If I mean maybe, I'll say

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:43.906
<v Senior Policy Specialist>maybe.

00:01:44.256 --> 00:01:46.704
<v Policy Analyst>Plain and kind. That works for me.
